        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mau movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Samoa, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>, Savai'i and the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> in the Western Islands; a separate Mau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a>, American Samoa; leaders exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mau a Pule on Savai'i from 1908; Lauaki and other leaders exiled 1909; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> annexation 1914; O le Mau formed 1926; height of activity in the late 1920s and early 1930s; Black Saturday in December 1929; truce 12 March 1930; Samoan Fono opened 1936; independence 1962</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Lauaki Namulau'ulu Mamoe, founder of the Mau a Pule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson">Olaf Frederick Nelson</a>, merchant of mixed Swedish and Samoan heritage; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a>, paramount chief killed on Black Saturday; Luafalemana Moeono Taele, matua of Falefa; Alfred Smyth and Edwin Gurr, part-European leaders; Samuel Sailele Ripley, leader of the American Samoa Mau</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">the movement's efforts ultimately resulted in the political independence of Samoa in 1962; in 2002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Prime Minister apologised for the colonial treatment of Samoans; the American Samoa Mau was totally suppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></div>
