    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mau means 'resolute' in Samoan, and its motto was Samoa mo Samoa (Samoa for the Samoans); it built on village committees of chiefs and talking men, and by the mid- to late 1920s around 85% of Samoans were involved in open resistance.</li><li>Its methods were boycotts, refusal to pay taxes, picketing and mass arrests in which prisoners demanded trial, and on Black Saturday in December 1929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> military police fired on a procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a> among others.</li><li>Western Samoa became independent in 1962, with Tupua Tamasese Meaole, brother of the slain Mau leader, as co-head of state; in 2002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark">Helen Clark</a> apologised for the colonial treatment of Samoans.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the Mau were arrested in such numbers that there were too few jail cells, the prisoners came and went as they pleased; one group held in a three-sided 'cell' facing the ocean swam away to tend their gardens and visit their families.</p></aside>
    </section>

