    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">A dispute between the German colonial administration and the Samoan Council of Chiefs over a native-owned copra business leads to the formation of the Mau a Pule on Savai'i</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1909</span><span class="rf-text">Lauaki and the other senior leaders of the Mau a Pule are exiled to the German colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> forces annex Western Samoa without German opposition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">After Olaf Nelson's lobbying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, two public meetings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> form the Samoan League, O le Mau</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> military police fire on a procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> on Black Saturday, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text">A truce is declared on 12 March, with the male Mau members keeping their right to non-cooperation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">A Samoan Fono with elected representatives is opened at Mulinu'u after the election of a Labour Government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1962</span><span class="rf-text">Western Samoa gains its independence</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Solf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf">Wilhelm Solf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R73059,_Wilhelm_Solf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The history of the Mau movement can be seen as beginning in the 19th century, with European contact and the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, each vying for control of the Pacific nation. Samoa became German Samoa (1900–1914). In 1908 a dispute arose between the German colonial administration and the Malo o Samoa, the Samoan Council of Chiefs, over the establishment of a copra business owned and controlled by native Samoans. It led to the formation of a resistance movement, the Mau a Pule, on Savai'i by Lauaki Namulau'ulu Mamoe, a leader from Safotulafai who was deposed by the German Governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf">Wilhelm Solf</a>.</p><p>Solf deposed members of the Malo o Samoa and called in two German warships as a show of strength. He then arranged a 'peace talk' on a German ship, on condition that Lauaki first disperse his army, and Lauaki and some of the chiefs were betrayed there. In 1909 Lauaki and the other senior leaders were exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a>, where they stayed until 1914. Many died before returning, and Lauaki died on the way back to Samoa in 1915. In 1914, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> forces annexed Western Samoa, unopposed by the Germans.</p><p>Military rule by New Zealand continued after the war. An influenza epidemic then killed some 7,500 Samoans, around 22 per cent of the population, after the ship SS Talune was allowed to dock at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> with its crew and passengers obviously sick. No attempt was made to contain the spread, whereas American Samoa, which had taken quarantine precautions, had no deaths. This catastrophe laid a new foundation for discontent with an administration many Samoans already thought incompetent and dishonest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson.jpg" alt="Olaf Frederick Nelson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson">Olaf Frederick Nelson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta%E2%80%99isi_O.F._Nelson_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mau was a non-violent movement for Samoan independence from colonial rule. In Samoan the word means 'resolute' or 'resolved', in the sense of 'opinion', 'unwavering', 'to be decided' or 'testimony', and also denotes firm strength. Its motto was the words Samoa mo Samoa, 'Samoa for the Samoans'. Its petition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Government quoted an ancient Samoan proverb: 'We are moved by love, but never driven by intimidation.' Supporters wore a uniform of a navy blue lavalava with a white stripe, which the colonial administration later banned.</p><p>The grievances were those of an administration perceived as incompetent and dishonest, together with the slow and deliberate erosion of traditional Samoan social structures by successive administrators and a general failure to understand and respect Samoan culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson">Olaf Frederick Nelson</a>, one of the leaders, was frustrated by the exclusion of native and part-Samoans from governance, and in 1926 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> to lobby the New Zealand government for increased self-rule.</p><p>The movement's methods were forms of non-cooperation. Members neglected the compulsory weekly search for the rhinoceros beetle, an enemy of the coconut palm, which threatened the copra industry; when a per-capita quota was imposed, many villages bred the insects in tightly woven baskets instead of collecting them. Later the Mau boycotted imported goods, refused to pay taxes and picketed stores in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> to prevent the payment of customs. In 1931 news of the Indian independence movement reached Samoa and partly inspired the Mau.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii.jpg" alt="Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mau_leaders_and_Tupua_Tamasese_Lealofi_III_in_front_of_Mau_office_1929.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alfred John Tattersall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When the Minister for External Affairs, William Nosworthy, promised to visit Samoa and then postponed his trip, Nelson organised two public meetings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, attended by hundreds, and the Samoan League, O le Mau, was formed in 1926. It published the Samoa Guardian as its mouthpiece. In 1927 the administrator, George Richardson, changed the law to allow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of Europeans or part-Europeans charged with fomenting unrest; Nelson was later exiled with two other part-European leaders, Alfred Smyth and Edwin Gurr. A joint select committee admitted that a very substantial proportion of Samoans had joined the Mau, enough to paralyse the functions of government.</p><p>Richardson sent a warship and a force of 70 marines. About 400 Mau members were arrested, but others gave themselves up in such numbers that cells ran short, and those arrested demanded trial and then refused to enter pleas, rejecting the court's jurisdiction. The next administrator, Stephen Allen, replaced the marines with special <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> police and targeted the leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a> was imprisoned for six months for non-payment of taxes.</p><p>On Black Saturday, late in December 1929, New Zealand military police fired on a procession assembled to welcome home Alfred Smyth. Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III, calling for peace with his back to the police, was shot. Eight Samoans died that day, three later, and about 50 were wounded; one policeman was clubbed to death. Marines were sent in January 1930, a Samoan boy of 16 was shot dead running away, and a truce was declared on 12 March 1930.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mau's support came from the matai, the heads of families in Samoa's traditional structure, who used family and chiefly title connections to gather it. Each village that joined formed a committee of chiefs and 'talking men'; these committees became the basic element of an alternative system of governance. To win Savai'i's orators, the Tumua of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> sailed in a fleet of canoes under Luafalemana Moeono Taele, matua of Falefa, who had resigned from the administration's police force to join the Mau. Moeono recalled Falefa's support for the Mau a Pule and so persuaded Asiata and the assembled Pule to join.</p><p>Samoans of mixed parentage, facing discrimination from both cultures, also played a key role. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson">Olaf Frederick Nelson</a> was a merchant of mixed Swedish and Samoan heritage, the richest man in Samoa and well travelled; classified as a European, he considered himself Samoan 'by birth, blood, and sentiment'. He had lost a child, his mother, sister, only brother and a sister-in-law to the influenza epidemic. Alfred Smyth and Edwin Gurr were also part-European leaders.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a>, one of the paramount chiefs of Samoa, led the movement after Nelson's exile. As he lay dying he is said to have told his followers: 'My blood has been spilt for Samoa, I am proud to give it. Do not dream of avenging it, as it was spilt in peace. If I die, peace must be maintained at any price.' After Black Saturday the Women's Mau emerged within days, as men hid from the administration. Women hid and protected the men and carried on writing letters of complaint and organising protests; Nelson telegraphed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Labour Party that Samoan women were organising a movement 'unparalleled in Samoan history'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nelson returned to Samoa in May 1933 after five years in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. The administrator, General Hart, demanded that he be excluded from any fono with the Mau leadership, and the Mau insisted he should be one of its delegates. On 15 November 1933 police raided the Mau's headquarters at Vaimoso and Nelson's residence at Tuaefu, and eight chiefs at a conference of 100 were arrested, with seven more at Savai'i a week later. Six months after his return Nelson was convicted of three charges of being connected to the Mau and sentenced to ten more years of exile and eight months of imprisonment in New Zealand.</p><p>The treatment of Samoans had become a contentious issue in some New Zealand electorates in the general election of 1935, which Labour won. Nelson's exile was cut short in 1936. The election of a Labour Government in New Zealand that year was a turning point: repression was relaxed, and a Samoan Parliament with elected representatives was opened at Mulinu'u on 30 September 1936. There were further acts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>, including a sit-in in parliament over the payment of the representatives. Nelson helped sign a co-operation agreement between Samoan leaders and the administration and was elected to the Legislative Council in 1938 and 1941.</p><p>In 1946 a Fono of district representatives rejected a Trusteeship Agreement put forward by New Zealand, stating a desire for self-government. When Western Samoa became independent in 1962, Tupua Tamasese Meaole, younger brother and successor of the slain Mau leader, became its first co-head of state with Malietoa Tanumafili II. Fiamē Mata'afa Faumuina Mulinu'u II, son of the Mau leader Mata'afa Faumuina Fiame Mulinu'u I, became the first Prime Minister.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helen-clark.jpg" alt="Helen Clark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark">Helen Clark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helen_Clark_official_photo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Commission on Drug Policy, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The success of the Mau in gathering national support showed that fa'a Samoa was still strong despite colonialism. Its efforts ultimately resulted in Samoa's political independence in 1962, though the height of its activity in the Western Islands was in the late 1920s and early 1930s. In July 1997 the Samoa Constitution was amended to change the country's name to Samoa, officially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoa">Independent State of Samoa</a>. Scholars have called the women's movement that emerged after Black Saturday a successful one.</p><p>In 2002, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> during the 40th anniversary of independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark">Helen Clark</a> made an unprecedented move and apologised to Samoa for New Zealand's treatment of Samoans in the colonial era. The apology covered the influenza epidemic, the shooting of unarmed Mau protesters by New Zealand police in 1929 and the banishing of matai from their homes.</p><p>A Mau also arose in American Samoa, in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexation of the eastern islands in 1899. In the 1920s on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> it signed petitions and resisted the taxation of copra, in an open affront to the American Navy. Its leader, Samuel Sailele Ripley of Leone, was barred from disembarking on his return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and never allowed home; he later became mayor of Richmond, California. A committee sent in 1930 reported in favour of the American position, and the movement was totally suppressed, though its influence continued to be felt. A Samoan hip hop group founded in 1990 took the name The Mau and the motto Samoa Mo Samoa.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Mau was a non-violent Samoan movement against colonial rule that grew from the Mau a Pule of 1908 on Savai'i into a national movement in the 1920s, led by chiefs and part-European leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> police killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a> and others on Black Saturday in 1929, and Samoa became independent in 1962.</em></p>

